<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lewis Carroll</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1832">1832</a>–<a href="/years/1898">1898</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Charles Lutwidge Dodgson, Dodgson</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lewis Carroll was the pen name of Charles Lutwidge Dodgson, an English author, poet, mathematician, photographer and Anglican deacon, born at Daresbury in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheshire">Cheshire</a>. He studied at Christ Church, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, where he lived for most of his life. His Alice's Adventures in Wonderland (1865) and its sequel Through the Looking-Glass (1871) are among the most important works of Victorian literature, and his poems Jabberwocky and The Hunting of the Snark (1876) belong to literary nonsense. Alice Liddell, a daughter of Henry Liddell, the Dean of Christ Church, is widely identified as the inspiration for Alice, though Carroll always denied it. He died of pneumonia in Guildford in 1898.</p></div>
